### Title
Upgrade CA packages with conda 26.9.4.

### Why
Installing conda must upgrade CA packages that trust the compromised eMudhra
intermediates, even when aggressive package updates are disabled.

### Platforms
- [ ] Windows
- [ ] macOS
- [ ] Linux

### Prerequisites
Use a disposable environment for each channel and solver combination. Test both
defaults and conda-forge with each supported solver. Replace `<channel>`,
`<release-channel>`, and `<solver>` below with the distribution channel, channel
containing the 26.9.4 candidate, and solver name.

Start with `ssl_verify: true` and no `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE`, `CURL_CA_BUNDLE`, or
`SSL_CERT_FILE` overrides. Download the certificate chains and verification
instructions from https://emudhra.we-have-a-problem.com/chains.zip.

### Steps
1. Create and activate an environment with conda 26.9.3 and older CA packages:
   ```console
   conda create --name ca-release-qa --override-channels --channel <channel> conda=26.9.3 "certifi<2026.10.18" "ca-certificates<2026.10.19"
   conda activate ca-release-qa
   ```
2. Disable aggressive package updates:
   ```console
   python -m conda config --env --clear aggressive_update_packages
   ```
3. Install the candidate with the selected solver:
   ```console
   python -m conda install --override-channels --channel <release-channel> --channel <channel> --solver <solver> conda=26.9.4
   ```
   Confirm `conda list` reports `certifi >=2026.10.19` and
   `ca-certificates >=2026.10.19` for conda-forge or `>=2026.10.20` for defaults.
4. Follow the downloaded instructions to verify all 12 disclosed certificate
   chains against the installed certifi and ca-certificates bundles. Each chain
   must fail certificate verification because its issuer is untrusted.
5. Confirm normal HTTPS operations still work. Before each command, set
   `CONDA_PKGS_DIRS` to a new empty directory so package, repodata, and shard
   caches cannot satisfy the request. Use verbose logs to confirm actual HTTPS
   requests for repodata and a package archive:
   ```console
   python -m conda search -vvv --override-channels --channel <channel> zlib
   python -m conda install -vvv --force-reinstall --override-channels --channel <channel> --solver <solver> zlib
   ```
   Run with `repodata_use_shards: false` for both channels and confirm a flat
   repodata download. Repeat with `repodata_use_shards: true` on conda-forge,
   using a solver that supports shards. Confirm requests for the shard index
   and individual shards. Enable the solver plugin's sharded-repodata option
   where required. Falling back to flat repodata does not verify shard fetching.

   Query a channel at `https://dv-g3b-emudhra.we-have-a-problem.com/` and at
   `https://dv-g3b-aaa-emudhra.we-have-a-problem.com/`. Both must fail certificate
   verification. A timeout, HTTP error, or missing package is not a pass.
6. Repeat the upgrade from a fresh 26.9.3 environment with an environment pin for
   `certifi<2026.10.19`, then separately for `ca-certificates<2026.10.19`. Each
   upgrade must fail to solve without changing installed packages.
7. Repeat the HTTPS checks through the supported proxy configuration. Check
   `ssl_verify: truststore` separately with an updated OS trust store.

### Pass criteria
The upgrade installs the required CA packages, incompatible pins prevent the
upgrade, compromised chains are rejected, and normal channel downloads work.

### Out of scope / notes
Custom CA files and OS trust stores need separate updates when selected by
`ssl_verify` or environment overrides. Updating base does not update every
existing environment. Pip's vendored CA bundle needs its own update. Track
feedstock, standalone, installer, and container updates in #16880.
